1
00:00:00,430 --> 00:00:02,870
A 15-foot wall of brown liquid came crashing

2
00:00:02,870 --> 00:00:03,950
through the streets.

3
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:07,810
It smashed buildings, swept away horses, crushed people,

4
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:10,150
and even damaged an elevated railway.

5
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:12,730
People tried to escape, but the liquid was

6
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:15,450
so thick and heavy that getting out of

7
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:17,130
its path was almost impossible.

8
00:00:17,130 --> 00:00:18,710
And the strangest part?

9
00:00:18,710 --> 00:00:20,650
This wasn't a flood of water.

10
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:22,290
It wasn't an avalanche.

11
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:24,070
It was a wave of molasses.

12
00:00:24,632 --> 00:00:27,592
On January 15, 1919, an ordinary afternoon in

13
00:00:27,592 --> 00:00:30,272
Boston turned into one of the most bizarre

14
00:00:30,272 --> 00:00:32,072
disasters in American history.

15
00:00:32,072 --> 00:00:34,832
And it all began with a giant storage

16
00:00:34,832 --> 00:00:36,512
tank that suddenly exploded.

17
00:00:36,512 --> 00:00:38,972
The tank stood in Boston's North End.

18
00:00:38,972 --> 00:00:41,772
It was around 50 feet tall and contained

19
00:00:41,772 --> 00:00:44,452
approximately 2.3 million gallons of molasses.

20
00:00:44,452 --> 00:00:47,532
But this wasn't just some giant container of

21
00:00:47,532 --> 00:00:50,672
pancake syrup. The molasses was used to produce

22
00:00:50,672 --> 00:00:54,432
industrial alcohol, which was needed for everything from

23
00:00:54,432 --> 00:00:56,192
liquor to wartime explosives.

24
00:00:56,770 --> 00:00:58,850
The company had built the tank just a

25
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:01,810
few years earlier, but there was a serious

26
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:03,430
problem. It leaked.

27
00:01:03,430 --> 00:01:06,130
In fact, molasses regularly seeped through its steel

28
00:01:06,130 --> 00:01:09,510
walls. Local children reportedly collected the dripping syrup

29
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:12,270
in buckets. And instead of properly fixing the

30
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:15,010
problem, the company painted the tank brown to

31
00:01:15,010 --> 00:01:15,850
hide the leaks.

32
00:01:16,383 --> 00:01:18,383
Then came that January afternoon.

33
00:01:18,383 --> 00:01:21,623
At around 12:30, people nearby heard a strange

34
00:01:21,623 --> 00:01:25,203
rumbling sound. Suddenly, the tank's steel walls began

35
00:01:25,203 --> 00:01:28,483
tearing apart. Its rivets burst out like machine-gun

36
00:01:28,483 --> 00:01:31,583
fire, and millions of gallons of molasses came

37
00:01:31,583 --> 00:01:33,843
crashing into the neighbourhood.

38
00:01:33,843 --> 00:01:36,923
The wave reportedly reached around 15 feet high

39
00:01:36,923 --> 00:01:39,383
and moved at an estimated 35 miles per

40
00:01:39,383 --> 00:01:42,183
hour. That's roughly 56 kilometres per hour.

41
00:01:42,706 --> 00:01:44,766
Imagine being on a street when a wall

42
00:01:44,766 --> 00:01:47,346
of thick, brown liquid comes rushing towards you

43
00:01:47,346 --> 00:01:49,486
faster than a car moving through a city.

44
00:01:49,486 --> 00:01:52,466
Buildings were knocked from their foundations.

45
00:01:52,466 --> 00:01:53,586
Horses became trapped.

46
00:01:53,586 --> 00:01:55,586
Wooden houses were smashed apart.

47
00:01:55,586 --> 00:01:58,786
The elevated railway was damaged, and an approaching

48
00:01:58,786 --> 00:02:00,986
train stopped short of the sagging tracks.

49
00:02:01,568 --> 00:02:03,528
And escaping the flood wasn't easy.

50
00:02:03,528 --> 00:02:06,628
Molasses is much denser than water, and its

51
00:02:06,628 --> 00:02:09,948
incredible thickness made it extremely difficult for people

52
00:02:09,948 --> 00:02:11,748
and animals to move through.

53
00:02:11,748 --> 00:02:14,628
The more some victims struggled, the harder it

54
00:02:14,628 --> 00:02:15,868
became to escape.

55
00:02:15,868 --> 00:02:20,548
Rescuers rushed in, including police officers, firefighters, sailors

56
00:02:20,548 --> 00:02:22,528
and Red Cross workers.

57
00:02:22,528 --> 00:02:25,468
But reaching people trapped beneath the sticky mass

58
00:02:25,468 --> 00:02:26,688
was a nightmare.

59
00:02:26,688 --> 00:02:28,988
By the time the disaster was over, 21

60
00:02:28,988 --> 00:02:32,328
people had lost their lives and around 150 were injured.

61
00:02:32,838 --> 00:02:35,098
But here's where the story gets even more

62
00:02:35,098 --> 00:02:38,078
disturbing. The tank hadn't suddenly failed for no

63
00:02:38,078 --> 00:02:41,998
reason. Investigators discovered that its construction was seriously

64
00:02:41,998 --> 00:02:44,858
flawed. Its steel walls were too thin, and

65
00:02:44,858 --> 00:02:47,318
the tank had not been properly tested before

66
00:02:47,318 --> 00:02:48,878
being put into service.

67
00:02:48,878 --> 00:02:51,838
The company had also ignored warning signs, including

68
00:02:51,838 --> 00:02:54,898
repeated leaks. After a long legal battle, the

69
00:02:54,898 --> 00:02:57,378
company was held responsible for the disaster.

70
00:02:57,914 --> 00:03:00,454
And the cleanup was almost as unbelievable as

71
00:03:00,454 --> 00:03:01,694
the flood itself.

72
00:03:01,694 --> 00:03:05,074
Workers spent weeks removing molasses from streets, buildings

73
00:03:05,074 --> 00:03:07,714
and wreckage. They used salt water to wash

74
00:03:07,714 --> 00:03:10,014
away the syrup and spread sand to help

75
00:03:10,014 --> 00:03:12,714
absorb it. Even the harbour turned brown as

76
00:03:12,714 --> 00:03:15,574
the sticky mixture was washed into the water.

77
00:03:15,574 --> 00:03:19,114
The disaster eventually helped push Massachusetts towards stricter

78
00:03:19,114 --> 00:03:21,774
building regulations and engineering oversight.

79
00:03:21,774 --> 00:03:24,554
Today, the Great Molasses Flood is remembered as

80
00:03:24,554 --> 00:03:27,854
one of the strangest industrial disasters in American

81
00:03:27,854 --> 00:03:31,094
history. Because sometimes, a disaster doesn't need a

82
00:03:31,094 --> 00:03:33,614
hurricane, an earthquake or a tsunami.

83
00:03:33,614 --> 00:03:36,334
Sometimes, all it takes is a badly built

84
00:03:36,334 --> 00:03:39,314
tank, millions of gallons of molasses, and a

85
00:03:39,314 --> 00:03:41,454
company that ignored the warning signs.
